---
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import BaseHead from "../base/BaseHead.astro";
import MobileLayout from "../base/layouts/MobileLayout.astro";
import MailCTA from "../components/MailCTA.astro";

export const prerender = true;

const v635 = await getEntry("sloks", "6/35");
const v647 = await getEntry("sloks", "6/47");
const v126 = await getEntry("sloks", "12/6");
const v1866 = await getEntry("sloks", "18/66");
---

<BaseHead
  title="Meditation vs Prayer: What the Bhagavad Gita Actually Says"
  description="Does the Bhagavad Gita teach meditation or prayer? Read Krishna's actual teaching on dhyana (meditation) vs bhakti (devotion), with the original Sanskrit verses and their meaning."
>
  <MobileLayout>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-8 space-y-10">
      <header class="space-y-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-orange-600 uppercase tracking-wider">
          Bhagavad Gita · Chapter 6 (Dhyana Yoga) &amp; Chapter 12 (Bhakti Yoga)
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 font-serif leading-tight">
          Meditation vs Prayer: What the Bhagavad Gita Actually Says
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          The Gita is often called a manual for both meditation and devotion.
          But Krishna draws a clear line between them — and the answer depends
          on where you are in your practice. Here is what the original Sanskrit
          actually teaches.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 font-serif">
          The short answer
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Meditation (dhyana) is the discipline that trains the restless mind;
          prayer and devotion (bhakti) are what meditation is ultimately for.
          Krishna doesn't ask you to choose one — he describes meditation as the
          path, and devotion as the goal. Both are means to the same end:
          a steady mind fixed on the Supreme.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 font-serif">
          The mind is the real obstacle
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          When Arjuna asks how anyone could control the restless mind, Krishna's
          reply is the Gita's core teaching on meditation:
        </p>
        <blockquote class="bg-orange-50/40 rounded-2xl p-6 space-y-3">
          <p class="text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-relaxed font-medium text-sanskrit">
            {v635?.data.text.replaceAll("\n", " ")}
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm italic text-gray-500 leading-loose">
            {v635?.data.transliteration.replaceAll("\n", " ")}
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            "The mind is indeed restless and hard to control. But by practice
            (abhyasa) and detachment (vairagya), it can be tamed."
            — Bhagavad Gita 6.35
          </p>
          <a
            href="/6/35/"
            class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-orange-600 hover:underline"
          >
            Read the full verse with word meanings →
          </a>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          This is meditation in the Gita's sense: not emptying the head, but
          steadying it. The two tools Krishna names are{" "}
          <strong>practice</strong> (returning the mind again and again) and{" "}
          <strong>detachment</strong> (not being dragged by whatever arises).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 font-serif">
          Where prayer fits in
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Krishna then crowns the chapter of meditation with a surprising
          verse — the highest meditator is not the one with the strongest
          concentration, but the one who meditates with faith and devotion:
        </p>
        <blockquote class="bg-orange-50/40 rounded-2xl p-6 space-y-3">
          <p class="text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-relaxed font-medium text-sanskrit">
            {v647?.data.text.replaceAll("\n", " ")}
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            "Of all yogis, the one who worships Me with inner devotion and faith
            is the most perfectly united with Me."
            — Bhagavad Gita 6.47
          </p>
          <a
            href="/6/47/"
            class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-orange-600 hover:underline"
          >
            Read the full verse →
          </a>
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          In the same way, Chapter 12 — Bhakti Yoga — opens by comparing
          those who meditate on the formless with those who devote themselves in
          love. Krishna's practical advice for most people is the devotional
          route:
        </p>
        <blockquote class="bg-orange-50/40 rounded-2xl p-6 space-y-3">
          <p class="text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-relaxed font-medium text-sanskrit">
            {v126?.data.text.replaceAll("\n", " ")}
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            "Those who dedicate all actions to Me and meditate on Me with
            undivided devotion — I swiftly deliver them from the ocean of
            repeated birth and death."
            — Bhagavad Gita 12.6
          </p>
          <a
            href="/12/6/"
            class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-orange-600 hover:underline"
          >
            Read the full verse →
          </a>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 font-serif">
          A practical way to combine them
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          The Gita's own answer is a rhythm, not a either/or:
        </p>
        <ol class="space-y-3 list-decimal list-inside text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <strong>Practice daily</strong> — even a few minutes of steadying
            the mind (Gita 6.35), the way the{" "}
            <a href="/6/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">sixth chapter</a>{" "}
            describes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Pray with all actions</strong> — offer your work rather than
            only your words (Chapter 12). This is devotion you can do while
            doing anything else.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>End with surrender</strong> — the famous final verse of the
            Gita:
          </li>
        </ol>
        <blockquote class="bg-orange-50/40 rounded-2xl p-6 space-y-3">
          <p class="text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-relaxed font-medium text-sanskrit">
            {v1866?.data.text.replaceAll("\n", " ")}
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            "Abandon all varieties of duty and surrender to Me alone. I will
            free you from all sins — do not grieve."
            — Bhagavad Gita 18.66
          </p>
          <a
            href="/18/66/"
            class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-orange-600 hover:underline"
          >
            Read the full verse →
          </a>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 font-serif">
          Study these verses deeply
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Read each verse with the original Sanskrit, word-by-word meanings,
          multiple translations and commentaries:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-500">
          <li>
            ·{" "}
            <a href="/6/35/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">
              Bhagavad Gita 6.35
            </a>{" "}
            — how to tame the restless mind
          </li>
          <li>
            ·{" "}
            <a href="/6/47/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">
              Bhagavad Gita 6.47
            </a>{" "}
            — the highest meditator
          </li>
          <li>
            ·{" "}
            <a href="/12/6/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">
              Bhagavad Gita 12.6
            </a>{" "}
            — meditation merged with devotion
          </li>
          <li>
            ·{" "}
            <a href="/18/66/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">
              Bhagavad Gita 18.66
            </a>{" "}
            — the closing call to surrender
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          Or study the full chapters:{" "}
          <a href="/6/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">
            Chapter 6 — Dhyana Yoga (Path of Meditation)
          </a>{" "}
          and{" "}
          <a href="/12/" class="text-orange-600 hover:underline">
            Chapter 12 — Bhakti Yoga (The Yoga of Devotion)
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="pt-4">
        <MailCTA />
      </div>
    </article>
  </MobileLayout>
</BaseHead>
